# Development Flow Rules - Go Clean Architecture

## Core Principle
**Always analyze the existing project structure before making any changes**. Every development task must follow a systematic approach: analyze → plan → document → align → implement → test → iterate.

## Pre-Development Analysis Phase

### Project Structure Analysis
- **Examine existing codebase**: Understand current architecture and patterns
- **Identify affected components**: Determine which layers, modules, and files will be impacted
- **Review dependencies**: Understand relationships between components
- **Assess current state**: Identify existing implementations and gaps
- **Check configuration**: Review current config files and environment setup

### Impact Assessment
- **Layer boundaries**: Ensure changes respect Clean Architecture principles
- **Dependency flow**: Verify no circular dependencies will be created
- **Testing impact**: Identify which tests need to be updated or created
- **Configuration changes**: Determine if new config parameters are needed
- **Documentation updates**: Plan necessary documentation changes

### Existing Structure Compatibility
- **Follow established patterns**: Use existing naming conventions and file organization
- **Respect layer responsibilities**: Maintain clear separation of concerns
- **Leverage existing infrastructure**: Use current config, logging, and validation systems
- **Preserve architectural integrity**: Don't break established patterns without justification

## Requirements Clarification Phase

### Active Questioning
- **Ask clarifying questions**: Don't assume requirements - ask for specifics
- **Validate understanding**: Repeat requirements back to ensure alignment
- **Identify edge cases**: Ask about error scenarios and boundary conditions
- **Performance requirements**: Understand expected performance characteristics
- **Security considerations**: Identify security implications and requirements

### Requirements Documentation
- **Write clear requirements**: Document what needs to be built
- **Define acceptance criteria**: Specify how success will be measured
- **Identify constraints**: Document any limitations or restrictions
- **List assumptions**: Document any assumptions being made
- **Define scope boundaries**: Clearly define what is in and out of scope

## Planning and Documentation Phase

### Technical Documentation
- **Create implementation plan**: Document the technical approach
- **Design architecture changes**: Plan any structural modifications
- **Define interfaces**: Design any new interfaces or contracts
- **Plan data flow**: Document how data will flow through the system
- **Identify risks**: Document potential challenges and mitigation strategies

### Task Breakdown
- **Break into small tasks**: Divide work into manageable, testable units
- **Define task dependencies**: Identify which tasks depend on others
- **Estimate effort**: Provide rough estimates for each task
- **Set acceptance criteria**: Define success criteria for each task
- **Plan testing strategy**: Define how each task will be tested

### Alignment and Feedback
- **Present plan for review**: Share the technical plan for feedback
- **Incorporate feedback**: Update plan based on input received
- **Get approval**: Ensure alignment before starting implementation
- **Document final plan**: Keep the approved plan as reference

## Implementation Phase

### Incremental Development
- **One task at a time**: Focus on completing one task before moving to the next
- **Small, focused changes**: Make minimal changes to achieve the goal
- **Frequent commits**: Commit work frequently with clear messages
- **Continuous testing**: Test each change immediately after implementation
- **Documentation updates**: Update documentation as you implement

### Code Quality Standards
- **Follow established patterns**: Use existing code style and conventions
- **Write clean, readable code**: Ensure code is self-documenting
- **Handle errors properly**: Implement proper error handling
- **Add appropriate logging**: Include relevant log statements
- **Follow naming conventions**: Use consistent, descriptive names

### Testing at Each Step
- **Unit tests**: Write tests for each new function or method
- **Integration tests**: Test component interactions
- **Run existing tests**: Ensure no regressions are introduced
- **Manual testing**: Verify functionality works as expected
- **Performance testing**: Validate performance characteristics

## Validation and Iteration Phase

### Comprehensive Testing
- **Run full test suite**: Execute all tests to ensure no regressions
- **Integration testing**: Test with other components
- **End-to-end testing**: Verify complete user workflows
- **Error scenario testing**: Test error conditions and edge cases
- **Performance validation**: Ensure performance requirements are met

### Code Review Process
- **Self-review**: Review your own code before considering it complete
- **Documentation review**: Ensure documentation is accurate and complete
- **Test coverage review**: Verify adequate test coverage
- **Architecture review**: Ensure architectural principles are maintained
- **Security review**: Check for potential security issues

### Iteration and Refinement
- **Address feedback**: Incorporate any feedback received
- **Refactor if needed**: Improve code quality based on review
- **Update documentation**: Keep documentation current
- **Re-test after changes**: Verify changes don't break existing functionality
- **Final validation**: Ensure all requirements are met

## Communication Guidelines

### Before Starting Work
- **Present analysis**: Share findings from project analysis
- **Show impact assessment**: Explain what will be affected
- **Propose approach**: Present technical approach for review
- **Ask for feedback**: Request input on the proposed plan
- **Get alignment**: Ensure everyone is on the same page

### During Development
- **Regular updates**: Provide progress updates on each task
- **Highlight challenges**: Communicate any issues or blockers
- **Request clarification**: Ask questions when requirements are unclear
- **Share decisions**: Explain technical decisions and rationale
- **Document changes**: Keep documentation updated

### After Completion
- **Present results**: Show what was accomplished
- **Demonstrate functionality**: Show the working implementation
- **Review test results**: Share test outcomes and coverage
- **Document lessons learned**: Capture insights for future reference
- **Plan next steps**: Identify any follow-up work needed

## Anti-patterns to Avoid

### Analysis Anti-patterns
- ❌ **Jumping into coding**: Starting without understanding the codebase
- ❌ **Ignoring existing patterns**: Not following established conventions
- ❌ **Not asking questions**: Assuming requirements without clarification
- ❌ **Skipping impact assessment**: Not understanding what will be affected
- ❌ **Ignoring architecture**: Breaking established architectural patterns

### Planning Anti-patterns
- ❌ **No documentation**: Not documenting the plan or approach
- ❌ **Big bang development**: Trying to implement everything at once
- ❌ **No task breakdown**: Not dividing work into manageable pieces
- ❌ **No feedback loop**: Not seeking input on the plan
- ❌ **Unclear requirements**: Proceeding with vague requirements

### Implementation Anti-patterns
- ❌ **No testing**: Implementing without writing tests
- ❌ **Breaking existing functionality**: Introducing regressions
- ❌ **Poor code quality**: Writing code that doesn't meet standards
- ❌ **No documentation updates**: Not keeping documentation current
- ❌ **Ignoring feedback**: Not incorporating review feedback

## Quality Gates

### Before Starting Implementation
- [ ] Project analysis completed
- [ ] Requirements clarified and documented
- [ ] Technical plan created and reviewed
- [ ] Tasks broken down and estimated
- [ ] Alignment obtained from stakeholders
- [ ] Testing strategy defined

### During Implementation
- [ ] Each task completed and tested
- [ ] Code follows established patterns
- [ ] Tests written and passing
- [ ] Documentation updated
- [ ] No regressions introduced
- [ ] Performance requirements met

### Before Completion
- [ ] All requirements implemented
- [ ] Full test suite passing
- [ ] Code review completed
- [ ] Documentation complete and accurate
- [ ] Performance validated
- [ ] Security review completed

## Continuous Improvement

### Process Refinement
- **Retrospectives**: Regular reviews of the development process
- **Feedback collection**: Gather feedback on the development approach
- **Process updates**: Continuously improve the development workflow
- **Tool evaluation**: Assess and improve development tools
- **Knowledge sharing**: Share lessons learned with the team

### Skill Development
- **Learning opportunities**: Identify areas for skill improvement
- **Best practices**: Stay updated with industry best practices
- **Code review skills**: Improve ability to review and provide feedback
- **Testing skills**: Enhance testing and quality assurance capabilities
- **Documentation skills**: Improve technical writing and documentation

